title: Genetics & Evolution
subject: Biology
level: GCSE
emoji: 🧬
description: DNA, inheritance, variation and natural selection.
---
What is DNA's shape called? :: A double helix.
What are the four bases in DNA? :: Adenine, thymine, cytosine and guanine.
Which bases pair together in DNA? :: A with T; C with G.
What is a gene? :: A short section of DNA that codes for a particular protein.
What is the genome? :: The entire genetic material of an organism.
What is an allele? :: A different version of the same gene.
What does dominant mean? :: The allele that is expressed even if only one copy is present.
What does homozygous mean? :: Having two identical alleles for a gene.
What does heterozygous mean? :: Having two different alleles for a gene.
What is a genotype? :: The combination of alleles an organism has.
What is a phenotype? :: The observable characteristics that result from the genotype and environment.
Cross Bb × Bb: what ratio of dominant to recessive phenotype? :: 3 : 1.
How many chromosomes are in a normal human body cell? :: 46 (23 pairs).
How many chromosomes are in a human gamete? :: 23.
Which chromosomes determine sex in humans? :: XX = female, XY = male.
Name a genetic disorder caused by a recessive allele. :: Cystic fibrosis.
What is natural selection? :: Individuals with beneficial characteristics are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on those alleles.
Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection? :: Charles Darwin.
What is a mutation? :: A random change in the DNA base sequence of a gene.
What is selective breeding? :: Humans choose parents with desired characteristics to breed together over many generations.
What is genetic engineering? :: Transferring a gene from one organism into another so it has the desired characteristic.
What is a species? :: A group of organisms that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring.
